📦 JAR (Java ARchive)
A standalone executable file

Usage:
You can run it directly with java -jar myapp.jar.
Ideal for microservices or applications deployed independently.

Deployment:
Doesn’t need an external application server (like Tomcat).
Spring Boot embeds Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow inside the JAR, so it’s self-contained.

📦 WAR (Web Application Archive)
What it is: A package designed for deployment on external servlet container. (like Tomcat, WebSphere)

Usage:
Traditional enterprise applications.
Useful if your organization already has a centralized application server.

Deployment:
Requires deployment into an external servlet container.

⚖️ Comparison Table
Feature	        JAR	                                WAR
Execution	    Run directly with java -jar	        Needs deployment on a server

Server	        Embedded (Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow)	External (Tomcat, Websphere)

Use Case        Microservices, standalone apps	    Enterprise apps,  centralized application server.


🚨 Key Takeaway
Use JAR: If you want a self-contained Spring Boot app that runs anywhere with Java installed. Perfect for microservices and modern deployments (Docker, Kubernetes, cloud).

Use WAR: If you must deploy into an existing enterprise application server environment.


📄 Properties File
Format: key=value pairs.
Simple and familiar to most Java developers.

📄 YAML File
Format: hierarchical, indentation-based.

🚨 Key Takeaway
Use application.properties if you prefer simplicity and flat configuration.

Use application.yml if your project has nested or complex settings — YAML is cleaner and easier to maintain.

Example Scenario: Multiple Datasources
Imagine you need to configure two different databases in your Spring Boot application.

Using application.properties:
properties
spring.datasource.primary.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db1
spring.datasource.primary.username=root
spring.datasource.primary.password=secret

spring.datasource.secondary.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/db2
spring.datasource.secondary.username=admin
spring.datasource.secondary.password=secret2

This works, but the flat structure can get messy as the configuration grows.

Using application.yml:
yaml
spring:
  datasource:
    primary:
      url: jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db1
      username: root
      password: secret
    secondary:
      url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/db2
      username: admin
      password: secret2
Here, the hierarchy is clear:

Easier to visualize relationships and maintain when configurations expand.



✅ Other Scenarios Where YAML Shines
Complex nested structures (e.g., multiple datasources, messaging queues, cloud service configs).

Profiles with overrides (different settings for dev, test, prod).


